{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_78","type":"agent_message","text":"# Review-round 1 continuation handoff\n\n**Ruler:** Félix Tshisekedi  \n**Country/year:** Democratic Republic of the Congo, 2023  \n**Continuation scope:** Chapters 2B–8B only  \n**Research date:** 17 July 2026  \n**Scoring:** None performed\n\n## Persistence status\n\nThe filesystem remained unavailable to tool execution. I therefore could not update:\n\n- `research-materials.md`\n- `research-ledger-manifest.json`\n\nThe manifest-ready additions, changed dispositions, and rejection records appear below for parent persistence.\n\n## Tool-access and source handling\n\n- MiniMax Search supplied discovery results.\n- Fetch opened underlying HTML pages and some PDFs.\n- Search-result snippets were not treated as evidence.\n- Several PDFs could be downloaded through Fetch but were returned as raw PDF streams rather than readable text. Those documents remain `locator_missing` unless a separate opened HTML summary supplied the claim.\n- Attempts to extract PDFs through local shell tools failed because the execution sandbox could not initialize its loopback network. No local files were inspected.\n- Brave remained quota-limited.\n- IMF HTML pages remained blocked with HTTP 403. The IMF report PDF opened only as raw bytes and therefore remains a gateway/document lead rather than a claim-level item.\n- No supplied structured dataset was re-fetched.\n- Chapter 1B was not reopened, in accordance with the reviewer’s saturation finding.\n\n---\n\n# New accepted evidence ledger entries\n\nThe original register ended at `WEB-026`. The following IDs continue that sequence.\n\n## WEB-027 — Direct presidential easing of the state of siege\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/10/13/actualite/politique/rdc-felix-tshisekedi-decide-dun-allegement-progressif-et-graduel-de|presidential address 12 October|gradual easing`\n- **Title:** *RDC : Félix Tshisekedi décide d’un « allégement progressif et graduel » de l’état de siège*\n- **Publisher/date:** Radio Okapi, 13 October 2023\n- **Factual claim:** In a televised address, Tshisekedi personally announced a gradual easing of the state of siege, restoration of civil authority in secured areas, removal of curfew and association/assembly restrictions, restoration of most civilian-court criminal jurisdiction, and payment of arrears to provincial officials.\n- **Locator:** Opening quotation and sections “L’amélioration de la situation sécuritaire” and “Les salaires des autorités civiles.”\n- **Supporting excerpt:** “J’ai opté, en toute responsabilité, pour la mise en œuvre d’un dispositif transitoire graduel au terme duquel cette situation exceptionnelle devra connaître sa fin.”\n- **Main points:** This is direct ruler-attributed correction/adaptation evidence. The change followed a national roundtable but did not terminate the state of siege or provide a completion calendar.\n- **Source type/confidence:** UN-supported local media; **medium-high**; final evidence for speech and announced measures.\n- **Period fit:** Direct target-period action.\n- **Attribution:** Direct personal declaration and instruction.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Tshisekedi claimed security had improved in some areas; continued insecurity and the later analytical assessment in WEB-028 challenge the breadth and novelty of the change.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.3, 2B.7; 3B.4, 3B.5, 3B.8–3B.10; 4B.3, 4B.4, 4B.9, 4B.10; 7B.2, 7B.8; 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.8–8B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-028 — Easing was partial, delayed, and difficult to operationalize\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.congoresearchgroup.org/fr/2023/10/20/pourquoi-le-president-felix-tshisekedi-a-t-il-annonce-l-allegement-de-letat-de-siege/|analysis of 12 October measures|limited novelty and no timetable`\n- **Title:** *Pourquoi le Président Félix Tshisekedi a-t-il annoncé l’allègement de l’état de siège ?*\n- **Publisher/date:** Congo Research Group/Ebuteli, 20 October 2023\n- **Factual claim:** The August roundtable recommended ending the state of siege, but Tshisekedi instead chose partial reclassification. The analysis found that curfew relief and partial civilian jurisdiction had already been announced earlier, restoration of civil authority had no timetable, and lifting the protest prohibition was one of few clearly new measures.\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs beginning “Les participants ont recommandé,” “Mais, il est difficile de voir,” and “La seule mesure.”\n- **Main points:** Provides an independent check on Tshisekedi’s claim of adaptation. It identifies political timing two months before the election and the Goma massacre as relevant context without proving electoral motive.\n- **Source type/confidence:** Research institute/academic policy analysis; **medium-high** by analogy; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Direct contemporary target-period analysis.\n- **Attribution:** Directly assesses Tshisekedi’s decision.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** The action did formally restore some liberties and civilian authority and responded to a participatory roundtable.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.4, 3B.5, 3B.8–3B.10; 4B.3, 4B.8–4B.10; 7B.8; 8B.6, 8B.8–8B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-029 — Presidential creation and repeated extension of the state of siege\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.amnesty.org/fr/latest/news/2023/05/drc-authorities-must-lift-state-of-siege-now/|background and findings|presidential authority and extensions`\n- **Title:** *RDC. Les autorités doivent lever l’« état de siège »*\n- **Publisher/date:** Amnesty International, 6 May 2023\n- **Factual claim:** Tshisekedi personally established the state of siege in May 2021, appointed military and police officials to replace civilian authorities, suspended elected provincial assemblies, and directed military courts to try civilians. By May 2023 the government had extended it over 50 times, while security deteriorated and military appointees used the powers to restrict rights.\n- **Locator:** Opening, paragraphs beginning “Pourtant, le gouvernement,” and “Complément d’information.”\n- **Supporting excerpt:** “Le président Félix Tshisekedi a instauré l’‘état de siège’… Il a nommé des membres de l’armée et de la police pour remplacer l’administration civile.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** NGO; **medium-high**; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** 2021 inherited policy deliberately retained during 2023; May 2023 continuation is target-period conduct.\n- **Attribution:** Strong, direct authority nexus: creation, appointments, and continued extensions.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** The declared aim was civilian protection and defeating armed groups amid a real security crisis.\n- **Mappings:** 2B.3; 3B.1, 3B.2, 3B.4–3B.6, 3B.8, 3B.10; 4B.3–4B.5, 4B.9, 4B.10; 7B.5–7B.8; 8B.1–8B.3, 8B.5–8B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-030 — Presidential notification and immediate response to Goma massacre\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/09/02/actualite/securite/tuerie-de-goma-felix-tshisekedi-consterne-exige-la-lumiere-sur-les|1 September cabinet|presidential report and sanctions instruction`\n- **Title:** *Tuerie de Goma : Félix Tshisekedi consterné, exige la lumière*\n- **Publisher/date:** Radio Okapi, 1–2 September 2023\n- **Factual claim:** Two days after the August 30 killings, Tshisekedi stated in cabinet that he was appalled and ordered the interior, defence, and justice ministers to report promptly on the circumstances so severe sanctions could be taken.\n- **Locator:** First four paragraphs.\n- **Supporting excerpt:** He demanded a report “dans un bref délai… pour que des sanctions sévères soient prises contre les responsables.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** UN-supported local media reporting an official cabinet statement; **medium-high** for the official act; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Direct target-period response.\n- **Attribution:** Direct personal response after notification.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** The initial official death toll was incomplete, and later sources found the investigation and command-responsibility inquiry too narrow.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.9, 3B.10; 4B.3; 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.7, 7B.10; 8B.8, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-031 — Immediate administrative measures after Goma\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/09/05/actualite/securite/repression-militaire-du-30-aout-le-gouverneur-constant-ndima-rappele|4 September commission measures|recall suspensions and victim support`\n- **Title:** *Répression militaire du 30 août : le gouverneur Constant Ndima rappelé*\n- **Publisher/date:** Radio Okapi, 5 September 2023\n- **Factual claim:** An interministerial commission recalled military governor Constant Ndima, suspended and recalled two senior police officers, committed government support for funerals and medical care, released one civic activist, and invited victim families to submit evidence.\n- **Locator:** Opening and bullet list of measures.\n- **Source type/confidence:** UN-supported local media reporting an official communiqué; **medium-high**; final evidence for formal measures.\n- **Period fit:** Direct 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Administration-level response following Tshisekedi’s explicit instruction in WEB-030; the source does not prove that he individually selected each measure.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Closing unregistered churches raises a separate freedom-of-religion concern; later inquiry remained narrow.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.7, 3B.9, 3B.10; 4B.3, 4B.4; 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.7; 8B.5, 8B.8, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-032 — Command nexus and limits of Goma accountability\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.crisisgroup.org/fr/qna/africa/great-lakes/democratic-republic-congo/massacre-goma-clouds-dr-congos-elections-and-un|presidential response and direct-command discussion|Goma`\n- **Title:** *Le massacre de Goma assombrit la perspective des élections en RD Congo et l’avenir de la mission de l’ONU*\n- **Publisher/date:** International Crisis Group, 15 September 2023\n- **Factual claim:** Crisis Group reported that Tshisekedi demanded clarification on September 1, senior officers were arrested, and the military governor was recalled. It emphasized that the Republican Guard was directly under Tshisekedi’s authority and questioned how such a large deployment could occur without superior oversight, while noting the rapid military trial as a positive step.\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs beginning “Kinshasa a pris conscience,” “Les événements du 30 août,” and final recommendations.\n- **Source type/confidence:** Conflict-analysis NGO; **medium-high** by analogy; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Direct contemporary 2023 analysis.\n- **Attribution:** Strengthens formal-authority attribution but does **not** establish that Tshisekedi ordered or knew of the operation beforehand.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** He opposed provincial attempts to justify the killings, demanded investigation, and initiated a fast response; political self-protection is presented as an analytical possibility, not a proven motive.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.1–3B.5, 3B.8–3B.10; 4B.3, 4B.5; 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.7, 7B.10; 8B.5, 8B.8, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Disposition change:** This source was rejected in the initial pass because Fetch returned 403; it opened successfully in round 1 and is now final evidence.\n\n## WEB-033 — Later review found no full command inquiry or victim compensation\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.hrw.org/fr/news/2024/08/30/rd-congo-peu-de-justice-pour-les-victimes-du-massacre-de-goma|accountability findings|limited investigation and no compensation`\n- **Title:** *RD Congo : Peu de justice pour les victimes du massacre de Goma*\n- **Publisher/date:** Human Rights Watch, 30 August 2024\n- **Factual claim:** Four soldiers were convicted, including Colonel Mike Mikombe, but investigators did not examine senior command responsibility, allegations of soldiers finishing wounded people, or the burning of the temple; no victim compensation had been paid a year later.\n- **Locator:** Opening, sections describing the trial, and paragraph beginning “Le dossier à charge.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** NGO with video verification and witness interviews; **medium-high**; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Retrospective evidence about the 2023 event and remedy.\n- **Attribution:** Establishes remedy limits at government level; it does not prove Tshisekedi personally blocked the broader inquiry.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Convictions occurred promptly; death sentences imposed on protest-group defendants were commuted to life imprisonment.\n- **Mappings:** 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.9, 3B.10; 4B.4; 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.7; 8B.8.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-034 — Direct presidential role in disputed CENI appointment chain\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.voaafrique.com/a/kinshasa-le-pr%C3%A9sident-tshisekedi-confirme-denis-kadima-%C3%A0-la-t%C3%AAte-de-la-commission-%C3%A9lectorale-de-la-rdc-/6282064.html|appointment procedure|Kadima confirmation`\n- **Title:** *Félix Tshisekedi confirme Denis Kadima à la tête de la CENI*\n- **Publisher/date:** VOA Afrique, 22 October 2021\n- **Factual claim:** Tshisekedi signed the decree confirming Denis Kadima as CENI chair after six of eight religious groups proposed him and the National Assembly approved him, despite opposition from the Catholic and main Protestant churches, which alleged pro-Tshisekedi bias.\n- **Locator:** Full article, especially first six paragraphs.\n- **Supporting excerpt:** “J’ai décidé de signer l’ordonnance désignant les membres du nouveau bureau de la Ceni.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** International public-service media; **medium-high**; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Pre-period institutional appointment directly shaping the 2023 election; baseline/attribution context rather than target-year action.\n- **Attribution:** Direct formal presidential appointment; the selection chain also involved religious bodies and parliament.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Kadima was an experienced election expert, and Tshisekedi publicly instructed CENI to organize free, transparent, timely elections. Allegations of closeness or corruption were disputed concerns, not adjudicated facts.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.4, 4B.10; 7B.5, 7B.6, 7B.9; 8B.2, 8B.4, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-035 — CENI appointment generated an independence and confidence crisis\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.dw.com/fr/rdc-c%C3%A9ni-kadima/a-59633340|appointment controversy|CENI independence concern`\n- **Title:** *RDC : les membres de la Céni ont prêté serment*\n- **Publisher/date:** Deutsche Welle, 26 October 2021\n- **Factual claim:** Catholic and Protestant churches disputed Kadima’s independence after Tshisekedi signed the appointment decree; civil-society and analyst interviews warned that the lack of consensus undermined confidence before the 2023 cycle.\n- **Locator:** Opening through “Un processus biaisé.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** International public-service media; **medium-high**; final/context evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Pre-period institutional baseline relevant to the 2023 process.\n- **Attribution:** Confirms direct formal action but not actual manipulation or personal control of CENI.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Concerns were predictions and perceptions at appointment time.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.4, 4B.10; 7B.5, 7B.6; 8B.4, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `context`\n\n## WEB-036 — Domestic observers found a clear presidential leader but localized integrity problems\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/12/28/emissions/dialogue-entre-congolais/elections-2023-la-moe-cenco-ecc-publie-son-rapport|preliminary observation findings|clear leader and irregularities`\n- **Title:** *Élections 2023 : la MOE CENCO-ECC publie son rapport préliminaire*\n- **Publisher/date:** Radio Okapi, 28 December 2023\n- **Factual claim:** The Catholic-Protestant observer mission reported that one presidential candidate clearly led with more than half the votes, while multiple irregularities could compromise integrity in affected places. It urged CENI to base provisional results on complete local compilation-center tabulations under election-law article 71.\n- **Locator:** Opening four paragraphs.\n- **Source type/confidence:** UN-supported local media accurately summarizing a large domestic observer mission; **medium-high**; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Direct target-year observation.\n- **Attribution:** Does not identify the leading candidate in the passage and does not attribute irregularities personally to Tshisekedi.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** The finding of a clear majority leader is significant contrary evidence to claims that irregularities alone necessarily reversed the presidential outcome.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.4, 4B.10; 7B.6; 8B.7, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-037 — Domestic final report identified persistent irregularities and weak enforcement\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.vaticannews.va/fr/eglise/news/2024-06/rdc-publication-du-rapport-final-du-cycle-electoral-2019-2023.html|final CENCO-ECC findings|campaign and enforcement`\n- **Title:** *RDC : publication du Rapport Final du cycle électoral 2019-2023*\n- **Publisher/date:** Vatican News summarizing MOE CENCO-ECC, 19 June 2024\n- **Factual claim:** CENCO-ECC found a tense campaign, premature campaigning by some candidates, propaganda left in place after legal deadlines without CSAC sanctions, direct and indirect election irregularities, and no appeal from Constitutional Court election judgments.\n- **Locator:** Sections “Un climat électoral tendu” and “Le plaidoyer.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** Reputable institutional media summarizing a domestic observer report; **medium-high**; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Retrospective direct assessment of the 2019–23 cycle.\n- **Attribution:** Institutional and candidate-level, not direct proof of presidential direction.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** CENI’s vice-president called the report partial/biased and pointed to digital engagement and a transparency center.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1–4B.4, 4B.6, 4B.8, 4B.10; 7B.6, 7B.8, 7B.9; 8B.5, 8B.7, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-038 — EU observer restriction remained technically ambiguous\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.africanews.com/amp/2023/11/29/drc-elections-eu-cancels-election-observation-mission-due-to-technical-issues/|EU statement|technical and security constraints`\n- **Title:** *DRC Elections: EU Cancels Election Observation Mission Due to Technical Issues*\n- **Publisher/date:** Africanews, 29 November 2023\n- **Factual claim:** The EU cancelled its nationwide mission because observers could not deploy for security reasons and essential communications equipment was unavailable due to technical constraints outside EU control; the EU explored maintaining a Kinshasa expert mission.\n- **Locator:** First seven paragraphs.\n- **Source type/confidence:** Reputable regional media quoting the EU diplomatic service; **medium-high**; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Direct 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Does not establish that Tshisekedi or his administration deliberately obstructed observers. It narrows the prior claim to a technically ambiguous failure.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Security conditions were independently severe; some expert observation remained possible.\n- **Mappings:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.10; 7B.6; 8B.5, 8B.9.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n- **Effect on earlier item:** WEB-009 should retain its factual account but explicitly incorporate WEB-038 as contrary evidence against inferring a deliberate “hidden agenda.”\n\n## WEB-039 — PDL-145T execution was 14.5% by August 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.mediacongo.net/publireportage-reportage-130927_pdl_145t_crefdl_appelle_l_igf_la_cour_des_compte_et_l_autorite_de_regulation_des_marches_publics_a_auditer_sur_l_utilisation_des_fonds_affectes_rappor.html|CREFDL December report|311 of 2143 infrastructure units`\n- **Title:** *PDL-145T : CREFDL appelle l’IGF, la Cour des comptes et l’ARMP à auditer les fonds*\n- **Publisher/date:** MediaCongo reporting CREFDL, 18 December 2023\n- **Factual claim:** CREFDL’s May–August citizen audit reported 311 of 2,143 planned first-phase infrastructure units delivered by the end of August—a 14.5% execution rate—18 months after government disbursement.\n- **Locator:** Paragraph beginning “sur un total de 2.143 infrastructures.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** Local media reproducing named public-finance civil-society findings; **medium**; final evidence, corroborated by WEB-040.\n- **Period fit:** Direct target-year implementation.\n- **Attribution:** Flagship presidential program executed by UNDP, CFEF, and BCeCo with government financing; low execution is not solely presidential.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** Agencies projected that 1,523 units could be delivered by April 2024; this was a forecast, not a 2023 result.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.6, 5B.7, 5B.8, 5B.10; 6B.2–6B.5, 6B.9, 6B.10; 8B.2, 8B.5–8B.8, 8B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-040 — PDL-145T financial-control and coordination failures\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.mediacongo.net/publireportage-reportage-130927_pdl_145t_crefdl_appelle_l_igf_la_cour_des_compte_et_l_autorite_de_regulation_des_marches_publics_a_auditer_sur_l_utilisation_des_fonds_affectes_rappor.html|CREFDL financial findings|exception procedures and untraced funds`\n- **Factual claim:** CREFDL reported exceptional procedures used to disburse $672.4 million, $162 million earmarked for rural electrification not traced with no project executed, agricultural-road works not begun for lack of financing, and nonfunctioning provincial/territorial monitoring committees despite $34.24 million allocated for monitoring.\n- **Locator:** Paragraphs following “Cette attitude du Ministère des Finances” and “Défaut de suivi.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** Local media reproducing named civil-society audit findings; **medium**; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Direct 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Government finance and program coordination nexus; does not establish personal diversion or Tshisekedi’s knowledge.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** CREFDL still described the program as a potentially valuable route to basic services.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.3, 5B.5–5B.7, 5B.9, 5B.10; 6B.4, 6B.5, 6B.9; 7B.5–7B.7, 7B.9; 8B.2, 8B.5–8B.8, 8B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-041 — Independent corroboration of PDL-145T delivery count\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.congo-press.com/economie/pdl-145t-sur-2-143-infrastructures-attendues-les-agences-dexecution-ont-livre-au-gouvernement-un-total-de-311-soit-un-taux-dexecution-de-145-a-fin-aout-2023-crefdl/|CREFDL findings|execution and exceptional procedures`\n- **Title:** *PDL-145T : 311 infrastructures livrées, soit 14,5%*\n- **Publisher/date:** Congo Press, 19 December 2023\n- **Factual claim:** Separately reported the same CREFDL findings: 311 of 2,143 structures delivered, 14.5% execution, exceptional procedures for $672.4 million, and an untraced $162 million rural-electrification allocation.\n- **Locator:** Full short article.\n- **Source type/confidence:** Local media; **medium-low** as a duplicate report; context/corroboration only.\n- **Period fit:** Direct 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Same limits as WEB-039/040.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.5–5B.7, 5B.10; 6B.2, 6B.9; 7B.6; 8B.5–8B.8.\n- **Disposition:** `context`\n- **Source concentration warning:** This is not an independent underlying audit family; both reports derive from CREFDL.\n\n## WEB-042 — 2023 diversification and jobs constraints\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/a07ce227-6995-5fbe-af65-0854e71add3b|DRC CEM metadata summary|business environment and diversification`\n- **Title:** *DRC Country Economic Memorandum: Pathways to Economic Diversification and Regional Trade Integration*\n- **Publisher/date:** World Bank, 26 October 2023\n- **Factual claim:** World Bank identified business-regulation, electricity, digital access, finance, taxation, fiscal decentralization, and value-chain constraints as impediments to private-sector growth, SME expansion, diversification, and job creation.\n- **Locator:** Metadata summary for “Democratic Republic of the Congo Country Economic Memorandum.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** Intergovernmental; **high** for assessment; final evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Direct 2023 structural assessment.\n- **Attribution:** Primarily inherited and institutional conditions; the recommended policy agenda overlaps Tshisekedi’s reform program but does not measure his personal contribution.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** The report also describes substantial mining and agribusiness opportunities.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.1, 5B.4, 5B.6, 5B.8, 5B.10; 8B.1, 8B.7, 8B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `final_evidence`\n\n## WEB-043 — Poverty data quality gap and high estimated poverty\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://datacatalogfiles.worldbank.org/ddh-published/0064942/9/DR0092393/Global_POVEQ_COD.PDF|2023 poverty estimate|74.5 percent`\n- **Title:** *Poverty & Equity Brief — Democratic Republic of Congo*\n- **Publisher/date:** World Bank, publication date not recovered\n- **Factual claim:** Search discovery identified a World Bank brief estimating 2023 poverty at 74.5%, down 4.5 percentage points, but the underlying PDF was blocked by robots/401 and no page or table could be inspected.\n- **Locator:** Underlying PDF unavailable.\n- **Source type/confidence:** Intergovernmental gateway; otherwise high if opened.\n- **Period fit:** Purported direct 2023 estimate.\n- **Attribution:** Outcome estimate only.\n- **Mappings:** Potential 5B.8, 5B.10 and 6B.2, 6B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `discovery_only`\n- **Reason:** `underlying_source_missing_precise_page_and_methodology`\n\n## WEB-044 — Social safety-net delivery through end-2022\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2023/02/10/providing-safety-nets-for-the-most-vulnerable-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo-incremental-steps-with-a-large-footpri|Results|STEP delivery through December 2022`\n- **Title:** *Providing Safety Nets for the Most Vulnerable in DRC*\n- **Publisher/date:** World Bank, 10 February 2023\n- **Factual claim:** STEP delivered cash transfers, cash-for-work, and related support to 530,000 beneficiaries through December 2022, disbursing more than $93 million; investments in 1,166 vulnerable communities benefited 5.8 million people. Implementation involved DRC’s Social Fund, Social Affairs Ministry, UN agencies, and donors.\n- **Locator:** “Results,” “Partners,” and “Looking Ahead.”\n- **Source type/confidence:** Intergovernmental; **high** for program outputs; final/context evidence.\n- **Period fit:** Published in 2023 but outputs end in 2022; inherited ruler-period context, not a 2023 outcome.\n- **Attribution:** Shared heavily among government technical agencies, World Bank financing, and international partners. It does not establish Tshisekedi’s personal direction.\n- **Contrary/mitigating:** The system remained donor-funded and was still seeking transition toward a government/multi-donor national safety net.\n- **Mappings:** 5B.6, 5B.8, 5B.10; 6B.2–6B.5, 6B.6, 6B.9, 6B.10; 8B.3, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.10.\n- **Disposition:** `context`\n\n## WEB-045 — Direct Tshisekedi launch of maternity coverage\n\n- **Canonical fact key:** `https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20230906-rdc-les-accouchements-gratuits-avanc%C3%A9e-vers-une-couverture-sant%C3%A9-universelle|6 September launch|presidential maternity program`\n- **Title:** *RDC : les accouchements gratuits, avancée