No target-period, well-documented personal false statement and correction was located. Viral election misinformation concerned CENI and social-media claims rather than a verified Tshisekedi statement.\n\n---\n\n# Chapter continuation results\n\n## 2B — International peace and security\n\n### New yield\n\nRelevant new items: WEB-027, WEB-029, WEB-032, plus unresolved UN lead on FARDC/Wazalendo.\n\n### Improvements\n\n- **Presidential authority:** WEB-029 directly establishes that Tshisekedi created the military-governance system and appointed its authorities. This materially improves the prior administration-only attribution.\n- **Corrective action:** WEB-027 directly establishes his October decision to ease that system.\n- **Contrary evidence:** WEB-032 preserves his rapid response to Goma and the defensive-security context.\n- **Proxy lead:** The UN Group of Experts search lead specifically identifies FARDC support to Wazalendo in November 2023, but the underlying page could not be extracted and therefore remains excluded.\n\n### Gaps not improved sufficiently\n\n- Operational orders and international-humanitarian-law rules of engagement.\n- Evidence that Tshisekedi knew of particular Wazalendo abuses before continuing support.\n- Procurement, military patronage, and purpose of spending.\n- Primary AU/UN ceasefire chronology at document-page precision.\n- Accuracy of his public claims about Rwanda, M23, and internal destabilization.\n\n### Saturation assessment\n\nThe attribution picture improved around the state of siege and Goma response, but the proxy-control and international-diplomacy gaps remain open. Additional research would need accessible UN Group of Experts pages, AU communiqués, military directives, or parliamentary procurement records—not more generic conflict reporting.\n\n---\n\n## 3B — Domestic safety\n\n### New yield\n\nWEB-027–WEB-033 add seven source-claim units, including direct presidential acts and a command-authority assessment.\n\n### Improvements\n\n- **Notification:** WEB-030 proves Tshisekedi was informed by September 1 and personally ordered a report and sanctions.\n- **Administrative response:** WEB-031 identifies recalls, suspensions, victim support, and release of an activist.\n- **Command attribution:** WEB-032 establishes that the Republican Guard was formally under his direct authority while carefully withholding any claim that he ordered the killings.\n- **Remedy limits:** WEB-033 shows that the inquiry stopped below senior-command responsibility and compensation did not materialize.\n- **State-of-siege correction:** WEB-027 and WEB-028 provide direct action plus independent evaluation of its limited novelty.\n- **National emergency-power responsibility:** WEB-029 establishes presidential creation, appointments, and continued extensions.\n\n### Contrary evidence\n\n- Tshisekedi rejected early justifications, demanded clarification, and enabled rapid prosecutions.\n- Four soldiers were convicted.\n- Restrictions were partially eased in October.\n- A police officer was killed by protest-group members.\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\n- Accessible primary BCNUDH incident tables.\n- National torture and prison-condition data with exact pages.\n- Whether Tshisekedi followed up after learning that the command investigation was narrow.\n- Independent measurement of real-world effects after October easing.\n- Direct ruler-specific protection of women, children, displaced people, and minorities outside program design.\n\n### Saturation assessment\n\nThe reviewer’s most important 3B attribution gap was materially improved. Further work would chiefly refine national prevalence and post-remedy follow-through rather than the Goma/state-of-siege causal chain.\n\n---\n\n## 4B — Political freedom\n\n### New yield\n\nWEB-034–WEB-038 add appointment-chain, domestic-observer, and observer-restriction evidence.\n\n### Improvements\n\n- **CENI chain:** WEB-034 directly proves Tshisekedi signed Kadima’s appointment after