tually received free childbirth care and identifies reimbursement, consultation, budget, and service-package defects plus an inspection response.\n- **Social protection:** WEB-044 gives large, independently reported STEP delivery outputs, though they end in 2022 and are heavily donor-attributed.\n- **Disability:** WEB-047 supplies direct target-year action by Tshisekedi; WEB-048 identifies a dedicated ministry, policy, inclusion strategy, and UN partnership.\n- **Durability:** Both STEP and maternity evidence show significant donor dependence and weak domestic financing/administrative capacity.\n\n### Domains not materially improved\n\n- Housing policy.\n- Water and sanitation outcomes.\n- Nutrition outcomes.\n- Target-year mortality change.\n- Learning and school-completion outcomes.\n- Politicized or discriminatory welfare allocation.\n\nThe World Bank’s current country page contains education and health outputs, but they extend into 2023–24 and 2026 and do not isolate the immutable target year. They were not added as target-period evidence.\n\n### Saturation assessment\n\nThe chapter is no longer confined solely to education and maternity design: it now includes social protection and disability institutions, plus early maternity delivery. Outcome coverage remains sparse outside those areas.\n\n---\n\n## 7B — Personal integrity\n\n### New yield\n\nPotentially relevant additions: WEB-034, WEB-039–WEB-041, WEB-049. All require strict limits.\n\n### Improvements\n\n- **Appointment conduct:** Tshisekedi knowingly confirmed a contested CENI chair despite major church opposition; this proves a personal appointment decision but not dishonesty or corrupt intent.\n- **PDL controls:** CREFDL documented alleged fiscal-control failures and untraced program funds; this establishes a major scrutiny issue at government level but not personal enrichment.\n- **Institutional environment:** UK assessment confirms pervasive patronage and weak enforcement but remains country context.\n- **Exculpatory absence:** No verified 2023 personal enrichment finding, beneficial-ownership record, or adjudicated personal conflict was found.\n- **False statements:** No defensible specific false-statement/correction episode was found.\n\n### Personal-nexus assessment\n\nThe central reviewer concern remains. Round 1 did **not** locate evidence sufficient to transform national corruption or program-control failures into personal misconduct by Tshisekedi.\n\nThe strongest direct personal acts now are:\n\n1. the contested CENI appointment;\n2. public security-rights rhetoric from the original pass;\n3. prompt Goma investigation instruction;\n4. partial easing of emergency rule;\n5. direct welfare/disability-program launches.\n\nThese reveal choices and response patterns but do not establish personal enrichment, knowing deception, or obstruction for personal benefit.\n\n### Remaining gaps\n\n- 2023 public asset declaration and beneficial ownership.\n- Family/procurement links supported by primary records.\n- Verified truthfulness/correction episodes.\n- Response to IGF/CREFDL findings.\n- Nepotism/clientelism with appointment and benefit documentation.\n- Independent exculpatory scrutiny records.\n\n### Saturation assessment\n\nTargeted English and French searches were performed across assets, family procurement, appointments, audits, whistleblowers, and fact-checking. Results were dominated by post-period allegations or country-level corruption. The personal-nexus gap could not responsibly be improved further in this round.\n\n---\n\n## 8B — Effectiveness\n\n### New yield\n\nWEB-027–WEB-032, WEB-034, WEB-036–WEB-042, and WEB-044–WEB-048 materially expand adaptation, coordination, and execution evidence.\n\n### Improvements by program\n\n#### Security\n\n- Tshisekedi owned the state-of-siege design and appointments.\n- He received evidence of catastrophic failure at Goma and ordered investigation and sanctions.\n- He later eased the regime, but independent analysis